#!/bin/sh
# mux-env_lib: the session pointers a pane must not start without.
#
# THE BUG THIS EXISTS FOR, measured on a real box rather than imagined. Every
# agent pane on a machine was running with no `SSH_AUTH_SOCK`: `git push`
# failed in every repo and `git fetch` failed in the four with ssh origins,
# while the box itself was healthy (the agent held the key and could sign, and
# an https fetch worked with no key at all). It read as broken configuration
# and it was a missing variable.
#
# WHY IT IS MUX'S TO OWN rather than a caller's: A PANE'S ENVIRONMENT COMES
# FROM THE SERVER, not from whoever asked for the pane. Measured: a client
# holding a perfectly good value still got the SERVER's value in a new window.
# So no caller can fix this from outside, however careful it is.
#
# AND AN ATTACH DESTROYS, which is worse than a gap and is the actual
# mechanism. tmux's `update-environment` copies the attaching client's
# environment into the SESSION, and copying an ABSENT variable records a
# REMOVAL:
#
#     session before : SSH_AUTH_SOCK=/tmp/GOOD
#     after attach   : -SSH_AUTH_SOCK
#     pane after     : UNSET
#
# One attach from a client with no agent turns a healthy session into a broken
# one, and a session-level removal MASKS the server global, so there is no
# lower layer to fall back on. Measured both ways. On the box this was found on
# it happened because the human was latched in from ANOTHER MACHINE while that
# box sat at its login greeter, so the attaching client was an agent-less ssh.
# Any cross-box attach does it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THREE TIERS, AND ONLY THE MIDDLE ONE IS MUX'S
#
#   NAMES       `envhooks.d/<VARNAME>`, one file per variable. The directory
#               LISTING is the set, so there is no separate list that can
#               drift from the validators, and `ls` answers "what does mux
#               manage here". Which variables matter is not mux's knowledge:
#               this fleet needs four, another environment needs others, and
#               mux holding the list would make every such environment a patch
#               to mux.
#   VALUES      mux's own generic hunt (`mux_env_resolve`): the systemd user
#               manager first, then the caller's own environment.
#   VALIDATION  the hook from tier 1, run with the value as $1. NOT mux's,
#               because the value is usually not the thing to check.
#
# WHY VALIDATION CANNOT BE A CLOSED VOCABULARY, which is the one design point
# worth reading twice. Of the four real variables, only ONE validates directly:
#
#     SSH_AUTH_SOCK    /run/user/1000/gcr/ssh    the value itself
#     WAYLAND_DISPLAY  wayland-1                 $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/$v
#     DBUS_..._ADDRESS unix:path=/run/user/.../bus  its unix:path= field
#     DISPLAY          :0                        /tmp/.X11-unix/X0
#
# Three of four need a TRANSFORM before anything can be checked, and two of
# those transforms need ANOTHER variable's resolved value. A vocabulary of
# kinds (socket, file, dir) cannot express that. So every name is resolved
# BEFORE any is validated, and the resolved set is exported to each hook under
# a `MUX_RESOLVED_` prefix: that removes the ordering problem without numbering
# or dependency declarations, and the prefix keeps a possibly-dead candidate
# out of the hook's own environment.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THERE IS NO CACHE, and that is a decision rather than an omission.
#
# The standing rule for this package is that SESSIONS COME AND GO AND MUX
# LIVES ON, and the numbers that follow from it are decisive here. Measured on
# the box above:
#
#     tmux server started      22:14:22
#     graphical session ENDED  22:30:32      <- sixteen minutes later
#     a pane in it             next day
#
# THE SERVER OUTLIVED THE THING ITS POINTERS POINT AT BY NINETEEN HOURS, and
# the dead session left its socket FILE behind, so a presence test still
# answered yes. Any value cached at server start was stale within sixteen
# minutes. A cache is precisely the thing that outlives the session that
# filled it, so mux re-derives and re-validates every time instead. The hunt
# costs about 5ms, which is not worth optimising into a staleness bug.
#
# AND A PRESENCE TEST ON A HANDLE IS NOT A LIVENESS TEST, which this file
# learned the hard way: an earlier repair validated WAYLAND_DISPLAY with
# `[ -e "$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/$v" ]` and it PASSED on a socket whose listener had
# been gone for a day. A unix socket outlives its listener. That is why
# validation is the hook's job and not a rule mux writes down: only the hook
# knows that this one needs a connect.

# --- WHICH NAMES -----------------------------------------------------------
# UNION WITH PER-NAME OVERRIDE, which is the overlay order layouts, themes and
# the latch hooks already use: $MUX_DIR wins over $MUX_SHARE for the SAME name,
# and a name present in only one of them still counts. So mux ships a working
# set and a user can replace any one of them or add their own, without either
# side having to know about the other.
#
# SHIPPED ACTIVE, not as an unwired library, and this is the one place where
# this package's "a shipped default that fails is worse than no default" rule
# does NOT apply. That rule came from shipping a LOCATION (`scan ~/src 3`),
# which is a claim about a particular machine and made `mux check` fail on
# every box that keeps work elsewhere. A validator is not a location: on a box
# with no ssh agent, `envhooks.d/SSH_AUTH_SOCK` finds nothing and mux does
# nothing, which is silent and correct. Shipping them UNWIRED would instead
# mean a fresh install still starts panes with no agent, which is the whole bug
# this file exists for.
#
# KNOWN GAP, recorded rather than designed around: there is no way to opt OUT
# of a shipped name. R3 asks for the set to be EXTENSIBLE and says nothing
# about shrinking it, and it is hard to name a harm, since R9 below means a
# session already holding a working value keeps it. If a reason appears, the
# mechanism wants thinking about rather than guessing at now.
mux_env_names() {
  for _end in "$MUX_DIR/envhooks.d" "$MUX_SHARE/envhooks.d" \
              "$MUX_DIR/envhooks.d/strip.d" "$MUX_SHARE/envhooks.d/strip.d"; do
    [ -d "$_end" ] || continue
    for _enf in "$_end"/*; do
      # `-f` is what keeps `strip.d` itself out of the name list: a
      # directory is not a file, so the subdirectory cannot be mistaken for
      # a variable called "strip.d".
      [ -f "$_enf" ] || continue
      printf '%s\n' "${_enf##*/}"
    done
  done | LC_ALL=C sort -u
}

# --- DECLARED REMOVAL: envhooks.d/strip.d/<VARNAME> ------------------------
# THE USER'S DESIGN (2026-09-30), and the argument for it is not that it adds
# a capability. Stripping was ALREADY possible: a validator that exits 1 makes
# the value invalid, and invalid already means "do not write it, and clear it
# if present". Verified before this was built.
#
# WHAT IT ADDS IS THE DISTINCTION BETWEEN POLICY AND DIAGNOSIS, which matters
# because of what mux does with each. A validator saying 1 means THIS VALUE IS
# BROKEN, so mux acts and says so, loudly, every time, per R7. A deliberate
# opt-out expressed that way would therefore announce `dropped WAYLAND_DISPLAY`
# on every run forever, which is noise about a decision the human already made.
# A declared strip is not a finding. Same shape as this package's older rule
# that "I could not answer" and "the answer is no" are different claims.
#
# THE SAME SHAPE AS A VALIDATOR, deliberately: one file per variable, the
# directory listing is the set, $MUX_DIR overrides $MUX_SHARE per name. Anyone
# who understands envhooks.d understands this without being told.
#
# IT SEES THE VALUE, which is the half that makes it more than a list of names:
#
#     exit 0                              always; I manage this myself
#     case $1 in /run/user/999/*) exit 0  only values from a dead runtime dir
#                ;; *) exit 1 ;; esac
#
# 0 MEANS STRIP, because the hook answers "should this be removed?" and true is
# yes. That is the opposite of the validator next door, where 1 means the value
# is bad, and the asymmetry is deliberate: a file in `strip.d` whose answer of
# "no" meant "strip it" would be a trap for whoever writes the second one.
#
# AND ANYTHING ELSE MEANS KEEP, which is the only safe direction for a hook
# whose yes deletes data. A missing hook, one that errors, one that times out:
# none of them has made a finding, and the three-answer discipline the
# validators follow says a non-finding must not be acted on. Here that means
# never deleting on doubt.
mux_env_strip_hook() {   # <VARNAME>
  for _esd in "$MUX_DIR/envhooks.d/strip.d" "$MUX_SHARE/envhooks.d/strip.d"; do
    if [ -f "$_esd/$1" ]; then printf '%s' "$_esd/$1"; return 0; fi
  done
  return 1
}

# True when this name/value pair is declared strippable. Bounded and with
# stdin closed for the same reasons mux_env_validate is: the cost of a
# user-supplied hook is unbounded and this runs on the path a human types most.
mux_env_strips() {   # <VARNAME> <value> -> 0 strip, non-zero keep
  _esh=$(mux_env_strip_hook "$1") || return 1
  [ -x "$_esh" ] || return 1
  _est=$(mux_env_timeout)
  # THE if/else IS THE RETURN VALUE: its status is the status of whichever
  # branch ran, so there is nothing to test afterwards. Only an exact 0
  # strips; 124 from `timeout`, 126/127 from an unrunnable hook and any other
  # code all mean no finding was made, so the value is kept.
  if command -v timeout >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    timeout "$_est" "$_esh" "$2" >/dev/null 2>&1 </dev/null
  else
    "$_esh" "$2" >/dev/null 2>&1 </dev/null
  fi
}

# The hook for one name, $MUX_DIR first. Empty when there is none, which
# cannot happen for a name that came out of mux_env_names but can for one a
# caller asked about directly.
mux_env_hook() {   # <VARNAME>
  for _ehd in "$MUX_DIR/envhooks.d" "$MUX_SHARE/envhooks.d"; do
    if [ -f "$_ehd/$1" ]; then printf '%s' "$_ehd/$1"; return 0; fi
  done
  return 1
}

# --- TIER 2: THE VALUE -----------------------------------------------------
# THE SYSTEMD USER MANAGER FIRST, because it was right on both boxes including
# the broken one, where the correct values were sitting one command away the
# whole time while the tmux server, the window watcher and every pane were all
# wrong. It is also the layer that OUTLIVES the graphical session and the tmux
# server, which is what the no-cache argument above turns on.
#
# THEN THE CALLER'S OWN ENVIRONMENT, which covers a box with no user manager
# at all: if the shell running mux has a working value, that is a better answer
# than nothing. It is second because a long-lived shell can itself be stale.
#
# ONE CALL FOR THE WHOLE SET, not one per name. Measured: the hunt is about
# 5ms, and a naive one-read-per-variable cascade was 31ms.
mux_env_resolve() {   # -> VARNAME=value lines, for the names mux manages
  _erset=$(systemctl --user show-environment 2>/dev/null || true)
  mux_env_names | while IFS= read -r _ern; do
    [ -n "$_ern" ] || continue
    # `-n` and a leading anchor, so SSH_AUTH_SOCK cannot be matched by
    # something that merely ends with that name.
    _erv=$(printf '%s\n' "$_erset" | sed -n "s/^$_ern=//p" | head -1)
    if [ -z "$_erv" ]; then
      # The caller's own environment, read without eval: a variable name is
      # not a command and must never be treated as one.
      _erv=$(env | sed -n "s/^$_ern=//p" | head -1)
    fi
    [ -n "$_erv" ] || continue
    printf '%s=%s\n' "$_ern" "$_erv"
  done
}

# --- TIER 3: VALIDATION, WITH THREE ANSWERS --------------------------------
# THREE OUTCOMES, NOT TWO, and the third one is the whole point:
#
#     0        valid        use it
#     1        invalid      it is dead, do not write it
#     other    UNDETERMINED pass the value through unchanged
#
# The same 0/1/78 shape latch's hooks already use, and for the same reason
# recorded there: a hook that is MISSING, that ERRORS, or that RUNS OUT OF TIME
# has made no finding, and reading that as "found it dead" is the very sin this
# file exists to fix, committed by a different mechanism. "I could not answer"
# and "the answer is no" are different claims.
#
# NOTHING HERE MAY HANG, because this runs on the path a human types most. The
# cost of a user-supplied validator is unbounded and not mux's to predict, and
# this is not theoretical: the session that produced this design killed a
# background job that had been hung for FOURTEEN HOURS on a command blocking
# for input on a stdin that never reached EOF. For a session launcher that is
# the worst available failure, because the tool you reach for to fix things is
# the one that will not start.
#
# `< /dev/null` IS AS LOAD-BEARING AS THE TIMEOUT. A hook that reads stdin
# would otherwise consume the caller's, and with no terminal it would block
# forever in exactly the shape described above. The timeout bounds it; closing
# stdin means it never starts.
#
# THE BOUND IS A SEAM, environment then config then default, the same shape
# latch's five seams use, because an unusual environment may be willing to pay
# a second or two of startup for a validator with real work to do. Default 2s:
# long enough for a connect to a local socket and short enough that a wedged
# hook is not mistaken for a wedged mux.
MUX_ENV_TIMEOUT_DEFAULT=2

# The same three-layer read latch's `_seam` uses (environment, then
# $MUX_DIR/config, then the default), spelled out here rather than shared
# because latch's copy is a local function in a command and this is a lib. A
# NON-NUMERIC value is treated as absent rather than passed to `timeout`,
# which would otherwise fail per hook and turn every answer into
# "undetermined": a config typo must not silently disable validation.
mux_env_timeout() {
  _ett=${MUX_ENV_TIMEOUT:-}
  if [ -z "$_ett" ] && [ -r "$MUX_DIR/config" ]; then
    _ett=$(mux_conf_clean 2>/dev/null <"$MUX_DIR/config" \
      | awk '$1 == "env-timeout" { print $2; exit }')
  fi
  case ${_ett:-} in
  ''|*[!0-9]*) printf '%s' "$MUX_ENV_TIMEOUT_DEFAULT" ;;
  *) printf '%s' "$_ett" ;;
  esac
}

# Validate one already-resolved value. $2 is the value; the resolved set must
# already be exported by the caller (see mux_env_plan).
#
# NO `timeout` ON THE BOX IS NOT AN ERROR, it is one fewer guarantee: the hook
# still runs and still answers. mux only ever calls `timeout` behind
# `command -v`, because it is a harness dependency elsewhere in this package
# and must not become a product one.
mux_env_validate() {   # <VARNAME> <value> -> 0 valid, 1 invalid, 2 undetermined
  _evh=$(mux_env_hook "$1") || return 2
  [ -x "$_evh" ] || return 2
  _evt=$(mux_env_timeout)
  if command -v timeout >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    timeout "$_evt" "$_evh" "$2" >/dev/null 2>&1 </dev/null
  else
    "$_evh" "$2" >/dev/null 2>&1 </dev/null
  fi
  _evr=$?
  # `timeout` reports 124 for a hook that ran out of time, which is exactly
  # "made no finding" and must not be read as invalid.
  case $_evr in
  0) return 0 ;;
  1) return 1 ;;
  *) return 2 ;;
  esac
}

# --- THE PLAN --------------------------------------------------------------
# Resolve everything, then validate everything, then say what to DO. Emitting
# a plan rather than acting is what lets `mux check` report the same answer
# this would apply, without applying it: R7 asks for a withheld value to be
# visible, and a reporter that re-derives the decision separately is a second
# implementation that can disagree with the first.
#
# Lines are `<VERDICT> <VARNAME> <value>`, value possibly empty:
#
#     set    NAME value   resolved and valid
#     keep   NAME value   resolved, and the hook could not tell (R5)
#     drop   NAME         resolved and INVALID: do not write, clear if present
#     none   NAME         nothing resolved at all
mux_env_plan() {
  # EXPORTED BEFORE ANY VALIDATION, which is what removes the ordering
  # problem: WAYLAND_DISPLAY's hook needs XDG_RUNTIME_DIR's resolved value,
  # and neither hook has to declare that or be run in a particular order.
  _epres=$(mux_env_resolve)
  printf '%s\n' "$_epres" | while IFS= read -r _epl; do
    [ -n "$_epl" ] || continue
    export "MUX_RESOLVED_${_epl%%=*}=${_epl#*=}"
  done
  # A second pass, because the exports above happen in a subshell of the
  # pipeline and would not reach the validation below. Read from a here-doc so
  # a value containing whitespace survives, and so nothing is word-split.
  while IFS= read -r _epl; do
    [ -n "$_epl" ] || continue
    export "MUX_RESOLVED_${_epl%%=*}=${_epl#*=}"
  done <<EOF
$_epres
EOF

  mux_env_names | while IFS= read -r _epn; do
    [ -n "$_epn" ] || continue
    _epv=$(printf '%s\n' "$_epres" | sed -n "s/^$_epn=//p" | head -1)
    # STRIP IS ASKED FIRST, and about whichever value is in play. Here that is
    # the RESOLVED one, deciding whether to WRITE it; mux_env_apply asks again
    # about the SESSION's own value, deciding whether to REMOVE that. One
    # question, two subjects, which is why the hook takes the value rather
    # than only the name.
    #
    # ASKED EVEN WHEN NOTHING RESOLVED, because "mux, remove this" must work
    # for a name that has no value to find: the whole point may be that the
    # session holds something the login session does not.
    if mux_env_strips "$_epn" "$_epv"; then
      printf 'strip %s\n' "$_epn"; continue
    fi
    if [ -z "$_epv" ]; then printf 'none %s\n' "$_epn"; continue; fi
    mux_env_validate "$_epn" "$_epv"
    case $? in
    0) printf 'set %s %s\n'  "$_epn" "$_epv" ;;
    1) printf 'drop %s\n'    "$_epn" ;;
    *) printf 'keep %s %s\n' "$_epn" "$_epv" ;;
    esac
  done
}

# --- APPLYING IT: ensure a VALID value, never replace a working one --------
# R9, and it is not the same as "make every session agree". Converging every
# session onto one answer would make it impossible to run two sessions with
# deliberately DIFFERENT environments, and they would silently collapse into
# each other. The four variables that prompted this are box-wide pointers,
# identical for every session by nature, so nothing would break today; but the
# set is extensible ON PURPOSE, and the first genuinely per-session variable
# somebody adds would break. So the job is stated as: STOP A PANE STARTING
# BROKEN, not enforce uniformity.
#
#     session has no value, or -NAME  -> set it      (the poisoned session)
#     session's value VALIDATES       -> leave alone (someone meant that)
#     session's value FAILS           -> replace it  (the stale pointer)
#     session's value UNDETERMINED    -> leave alone (R5: no finding made)
#
# THE SESSION'S OWN VALUE IS VALIDATED TOO, not just the one mux resolved,
# which is the half that makes the table above more than decoration. It also
# means a `drop` in the plan does NOT automatically clear the session: the
# login session's pointer can be dead while a particular session holds a
# working one of its own.
#
# `-u` AND NEVER `-r` when clearing. `-r` is tmux's REMOVAL marker, the very
# `-NAME` that masks the global and started this whole investigation, so
# clearing with it would reintroduce the bug in the name of fixing it.
# Measured: `-u` clears cleanly.
#
# EVERY DECISION IS ANNOUNCED ON REQUEST, because R7 asks for a withheld or
# removed value to be VISIBLE. Silence here would make this a SECOND silent
# removal mechanism beside `update-environment`, which is exactly what made
# the original fault cost a long session to find: everything reported healthy.
# The caller decides where that goes; this function just says what it did.
# `-n` REPORTS WITHOUT ACTING, and it has to live HERE rather than in the
# caller. The first version had `mux update-env -n` print mux_env_plan
# instead, and that was wrong in a way worth recording: the plan says what
# RESOLVED and validated, while the decision to write also consults the
# SESSION's existing value. So the dry run listed two names and the real run
# changed three. A preview computed by a different code path from the action
# is not a preview, and the comment claiming otherwise was the giveaway.
mux_env_apply() {   # <session-or-empty> [-v|-n] -> what it changed/would
  _eas=${1:-}
  _eav=${2:-}
  _ean=0
  [ "$_eav" != -n ] || { _ean=1; _eav=-v; }
  mux_env_plan | while IFS=' ' read -r _eaverd _eaname _eaval; do
    [ -n "$_eaname" ] || continue
    # What this session holds right now. tmux answers three ways and they are
    # three different facts: `NAME=value`, `-NAME` (REMOVED, and the one that
    # matters most), or an error for a name it has never heard of.
    if [ -n "$_eas" ]; then
      _eacur=$(tm show-environment -t "$_eas" "$_eaname" 2>/dev/null || true)
    else
      _eacur=$(tm show-environment -g "$_eaname" 2>/dev/null || true)
    fi
    case $_eacur in
    "$_eaname="*) _eahave=${_eacur#"$_eaname="} ;;
    *)            _eahave= ;;
    esac

    # A DECLARED STRIP IS ANSWERED BEFORE VALIDITY, because it is a different
    # kind of statement: not "is this good" but "I do not want this here".
    # Asked about the SESSION's value, since that is what would be removed, so
    # a conditional hook can strip one value and leave another.
    if [ "$_eaverd" = strip ] || \
       { [ -n "$_eahave" ] && mux_env_strips "$_eaname" "$_eahave"; }; then
      if [ -n "$_eahave" ]; then
        if [ "$_ean" -eq 0 ]; then
          if [ -n "$_eas" ]; then
            tm set-environment -t "$_eas" -u "$_eaname" 2>/dev/null || true
          else
            tm set-environment -g -u "$_eaname" 2>/dev/null || true
          fi
        fi
        # `stripped`, NOT `dropped`, and the caller depends on the difference:
        # a dropped value is a FINDING and gets announced per R7, while a
        # stripped one is the human's own policy and must not be reported as
        # news on every run.
        [ -z "$_eav" ] || printf 'stripped %s\n' "$_eaname"
      fi
      continue
    fi

    if [ -n "$_eahave" ]; then
      # A value is already here. Judge THAT before touching anything.
      mux_env_validate "$_eaname" "$_eahave"
      case $? in
      0) continue ;;            # works: leave it exactly alone
      2) continue ;;            # cannot tell: no finding, so no action
      esac
    fi

    # Either nothing is here (absent or the `-NAME` removal marker), or what
    # is here has been shown to be dead.
    case $_eaverd in
    set|keep)
      if [ "$_ean" -eq 0 ]; then
        if [ -n "$_eas" ]; then
          tm set-environment -t "$_eas" "$_eaname" "$_eaval" 2>/dev/null || true
        else
          tm set-environment -g "$_eaname" "$_eaval" 2>/dev/null || true
        fi
      fi
      [ -z "$_eav" ] || printf 'set %s\n' "$_eaname" ;;
    drop|none)
      # Only worth saying, and only worth clearing, when something dead is
      # actually here: an absent variable needs no announcement.
      if [ -n "$_eahave" ]; then
        if [ "$_ean" -eq 0 ]; then
          if [ -n "$_eas" ]; then
            tm set-environment -t "$_eas" -u "$_eaname" 2>/dev/null || true
          else
            tm set-environment -g -u "$_eaname" 2>/dev/null || true
          fi
        fi
        [ -z "$_eav" ] || printf 'dropped %s\n' "$_eaname"
      fi ;;
    esac
  done
}
